The compiler already knows the built-in conversions.

A user-defined struct or choice can opt into a supported target by providing
the required method and declaring explicit conformance.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING
]

Now a `Label` can be cast to `String` at a typed destination.

This stays explicit. Matching method names alone do not create cast support.

### Numeric evidence

Builtin numeric conversions are decided by the static source and target types,
not by values the compiler happens to know. An integer conversion is exact when
the whole source range fits the destination, and fallible otherwise. `Byte` and
`U8` convert in both directions without failing.

Binary conversions may lose precision by design. Integer-to-float and
binary-float precision narrowing round to nearest, ties to even, and
float-to-integer conversion truncates toward zero and then checks the target
range. Narrowing a binary float fails only when the rounded result is not
finite, so precision loss alone is not a failure.

Evidence for the fixed-width, `Byte` and `Number` pairs is accepted end-state and
remains queued. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
